  "slug": "injury-cord-c274",
  "summary": "Arrayed Perturb-seq and syngeneic iPSC models now allow direct testing of microglial state regulation and immune cell trafficking.",
  "synthesis": "The three preprints share a focus on human stem cell-derived or single-cell-resolved models of immune cell states. Two studies address microglia directly. An arrayed Perturb-seq platform using the TF-iMG model maps microglial state regulation and target identification. A syngeneic iPSC comparison shows that microglia-like and CAM-like macrophages differ in TLR-driven NF-kB responses, HIV permissiveness and responses to cART. CellVADIS addresses a different scale, applying optimal transport to single-cell snapshots across tissues to infer immune cell trafficking, tissue entry points and T cell homing signatures. Its inferences are computational and extend the same cell-type-resolved logic to cross-tissue movement. The advance across these items is the pairing of controlled human cellular models with perturbational or inferential readouts of glial and immune cell state.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
